4. Single Source of Truth for Geometry

This requirement is critical.

The project must maintain one authoritative geometric model shared by the 2D and 3D views.

Never maintain separate, independently editable versions of the plan and the 3D building.

A change to the plan must update the model, which must then update the 3D representation.

The 3D scene must never write geometric changes back to the model.

Coordinate system

Use architectural world coordinates measured in meters.

Define the 2D coordinate system as follows:

- Positive X points to the right.
- Positive Y points upward.
- Angles are measured from the positive X axis.
- Positive angles rotate counterclockwise.
- Internally store coordinates and dimensions at adequate floating-point precision.
- Round values only for display, not during intermediate calculations.

The SVG renderer must correctly convert between world coordinates and screen coordinates, taking account of its vertical axis direction.

This conversion must work consistently at every zoom and pan level.

Do not store model coordinates in screen pixels.

Data model

Use stable, unique IDs for projects, walls, and geometric points.

A suitable conceptual model is:

Project

- ID.
- Name.
- Creation date.
- Last-modified date.
- Measurement unit.
- Default wall height.
- Default wall thickness.
- Point collection.
- Wall collection.
- Data schema version.

Point

- ID.
- X coordinate.
- Y coordinate.

Wall

- ID.
- Start-point ID.
- End-point ID.
- Thickness.
- Height.
- Creation metadata if useful.

A wall references two point records rather than owning duplicated endpoint coordinates.

Multiple walls can reference the same point ID to represent a real geometric connection.

This shared-point model is essential for reliable snapping and junction management.

Model integrity rules

- Every wall must reference two existing points.
- Every wall must have a unique ID.
- Both endpoints must be different points unless the geometry is explicitly rejected as invalid.
- Wall length must be greater than zero and meet a sensible minimum, initially 0.05 m.
- Wall thickness and height must be positive finite numbers.
- Invalid, missing, infinite, or non-numeric coordinates must never enter the saved project.
- Remove orphaned points after geometry operations when they are no longer referenced.
- Do not allow invalid references after deleting, splitting, or reconnecting walls.
- Every completed operation must leave the model in a valid state.

Implement a reusable model-validation function and invoke it at appropriate transaction boundaries.

5. Wall Geometry and Mathematical Accuracy

Implement geometry calculations in pure, testable functions.

At minimum, implement:

- Distance between two points.
- Wall length.
- Wall angle.
- Point projection onto a line segment.
- Point-to-segment distance.
- Point equality within a defined tolerance.
- Endpoint and intersection detection.
- Segment intersection calculations.
- Snap candidate scoring.
- Wall endpoint updates.
- Wall translation.
- Wall length adjustment.
- Wall angle adjustment.
- Point and connection cleanup.
- Wall geometry validation.
- Conversion between screen and world coordinates.
- Normalization of displayed angles.

For a wall with endpoints A and B:

"length = sqrt((Bx - Ax)^2 + (By - Ay)^2)"

"angle = atan2(By - Ay, Bx - Ax)"

Convert radians to degrees only for displaying or accepting degree-based input.

Avoid rounding geometry values after every drag event. Repeated rounding leads to cumulative errors and broken junctions.

The geometry module must not depend on React components, DOM events, SVG elements, or Three.js.

