The preview must update in real time without creating permanent model points on every pointer move.

Continuous drawing

Provide an optional, clearly controlled chained-wall drawing behavior.

When enabled, the next wall begins from the previous wall's endpoint after a segment is completed.

The user must be able to finish the chain or cancel the current drawing operation without adding unwanted walls.

Do not create a zero-length wall when the user taps twice at the same location.

Do not commit a wall that is below the minimum permitted length.

Provide clear Finish and Cancel behavior where appropriate.

Drawing feedback

Show:

- A temporary wall preview.
- Current length while drawing.
- Current angle when useful.
- A highlighted endpoint when snapping is available.
- A visible indication of which snap rule was used.
- A different appearance for a completed wall and the active preview.

The preview, snap markers, and dimension labels must not become part of the actual building geometry.

7. Reliable Snapping System

Snapping must operate on world geometry but use screen-space tolerances for usability.

The snap tolerance should initially be approximately 12–16 CSS pixels, configurable if necessary.

Convert screen-space tolerance to an appropriate world-space distance using the current viewport transformation and zoom.

Snapping must remain usable at both high and low zoom levels.

Snap targets

Support the following targets:

1. Existing wall endpoints.
2. Existing geometric junctions.
3. Intersections between wall segments.
4. Points along an existing wall when creating a valid intersection.
5. Grid points.
6. Optional orthogonal alignment at 0° and 90°.
7. Optional angle increments such as 15°, 30°, and 45°.

Expose simple controls for enabling or disabling grid snapping and angular snapping.

Endpoint snapping must be enabled by default.

Snap priority

When multiple candidates are nearby, use a predictable priority:

1. Existing endpoint or junction.
2. A valid wall intersection.
3. Orthogonal or enabled angular alignment.
4. Grid snapping.
5. Free placement.

Use distance, target type, and deterministic tie-breaking to avoid flickering between candidates.

Once a candidate is chosen during an interaction, the indicator should remain reasonably stable unless another candidate becomes clearly preferable.

Do not allow grid snapping to pull an endpoint away from a stronger existing junction snap.

Actual connections, not visual illusions

If a new wall endpoint snaps to an existing point, it must reference the same point ID.

If a new wall endpoint snaps to the interior of an existing wall:

- Calculate the exact intersection in world coordinates.
- Split the existing wall at that point into two collinear segments.
- Preserve the original wall's height and thickness.
- Preserve the existing wall's overall geometry.
- Create the appropriate shared point references.
- Keep the operation undoable as one transaction.

If the snap point is extremely close to an existing endpoint, use that endpoint rather than creating a tiny extra segment.

Do not display a snap indicator unless the final model can preserve the indicated connection correctly.

Snapping validation

Prevent accidental creation of:

- Tiny wall segments.
- Duplicate endpoints that should be connected.
- Visually overlapping but geometrically disconnected junctions.
- Duplicate wall segments.
- Unintended zero-length segments.
- Invalid intersections.

The user must be able to draw a connected sequence of walls without manually correcting tiny gaps.

8. Wall Selection

Selecting and editing a completed wall is a core feature, not an optional enhancement.

In Select mode, tapping or clicking a wall must select it.

The selected wall must display:

- A distinct selection outline.
- Visible start and end handles.
- Its length.
- Its angle.
- A clear selected state in the contextual inspector.

The selection must remain stable while the user edits numeric fields.

Tapping empty space should deselect the wall unless the user is performing a viewport-navigation gesture.

Tapping another wall must select that wall without affecting the geometry of the previous wall.

For reliable touch selection, use an invisible or visually subtle hit area wider than the visible wall itself.

The hit area must be sized in screen space so that a wall remains selectable even when zoomed out.

The hit area must not change the wall's actual thickness or geometry.

If two walls overlap visually, use a deterministic selection rule and allow cycling through overlapping objects or presenting a compact selection list when necessary.

Do not allow dimension annotations or selection handles to block the wall's own selection logic.

9. Independent Editing of a Selected Wall

This section overrides any default behavior that would unintentionally propagate geometry changes to neighboring walls.

Every standard edit must affect only the selected wall.

The application must not silently move, resize, rotate, or otherwise distort other walls because they share an endpoint with the selected wall.

Wall editing panel

When a wall is selected, display a compact contextual inspector containing:

- Length.
- Angle.
- Wall height.
- Wall thickness.
- Fixed Endpoint: Start or End.
- Optional precise position information if the available screen space permits.

All fields must reflect the selected wall's actual geometry.

All dimensions must use the project's selected unit and display it consistently.

A. Change wall length

When the user changes the Length field:

- Preserve the selected wall's angle.
- Keep the chosen fixed endpoint stationary.
- Recalculate the other endpoint.
- Update the selected wall's geometry.
- Recalculate its displayed values.
- Update the 3D view.
- Save the change.
- Add one undoable history entry.

Do not change the lengths or orientations of neighboring walls.

B. Change wall angle

When the user changes the Angle field:

- Preserve the selected wall's length.
- Keep the chosen fixed endpoint stationary.
- Recalculate the other endpoint using trigonometry.
- Update the selected wall.
- Update the displayed length and angle immediately after committing.
- Update the 3D scene.
- Save the change.
- Add one undoable history entry.

Do not rotate the entire plan.

C. Drag a wall endpoint

Dragging a start or end handle must allow precise editing of that endpoint.

During dragging:

- Show a live preview.
- Calculate length and angle continuously.
- Apply snapping.
- Keep the other endpoint fixed unless a different operation has explicitly been selected.
- Update the selected wall only.
- Commit one undoable operation when the drag finishes.

If the moved endpoint was shared with neighboring walls, detach it from that shared point before moving it independently. Do not move the neighboring walls.

If the endpoint is snapped to another valid point at its final location, connect it to that point.

D. Move the entire selected wall

Dragging the wall body must move the selected wall without rotating it or changing its length.

Both of its endpoints should translate by the same vector.

If either endpoint is shared with other walls, detach the selected wall's endpoint from the shared point before moving it independently. The neighboring walls must remain in place.

Allow the final endpoints to reconnect through valid snapping when they land on appropriate existing points.

E. Edit wall height or thickness

Changing height or thickness in the selected-wall inspector must affect only the selected wall.

It must update its 3D representation without changing unrelated wall properties.

Changing project default dimensions must affect newly created walls by default, not silently change existing walls.

If an Apply to Existing Walls action is ever included, it must be explicit and require confirmation.

Numeric input behavior

- Validate inputs before committing.
- Support decimal values.
- Prevent "NaN", infinity, negative dimensions, and zero-length results.
- Allow temporary incomplete text while the user is typing.
- Commit a valid input on Enter, blur, or an explicit Apply action.
- Reject invalid input without corrupting the model.
- Provide useful inline validation feedback.
- Avoid changing the geometry on every intermediate keystroke.
- Do not steal focus from a field during model updates.

10. Junction and Connection Rules

Connected walls create a special editing problem, so junction behavior must be explicitly defined.

Default policy: Edit Selected Wall Only

This is the default policy for all wall transformations.

When a selected wall is resized, rotated, or moved, neighboring walls stay where they are.

If an endpoint must move away from a shared point, create a new point for the selected wall endpoint and update only that wall's endpoint reference.

The old shared point must remain available to the other connected walls.

If the selected wall endpoint is moved back onto an existing endpoint and a valid snap is detected, it may reconnect to that point.

Required visual feedback

Show whether each endpoint is connected or independent.

If an edit disconnects an endpoint, give subtle, clear feedback.

Do not silently pretend that two endpoints are still connected if they are no longer geometrically connected.

Do not introduce automatic neighbor movement as a hidden side effect.

A future explicit junction-editing feature may move an entire connected junction, but it is outside the required first-version scope.

Connection integrity

Whenever connections are changed:

- Update point references atomically.
- Remove unused points.
- Preserve other walls' geometry.
- Prevent dangling IDs.
- Preserve wall dimensions.
- Recompute affected derived data.
- Support undo and redo.

For an operation that splits a wall at an intersection, preserve its original geometry and make the split part of the same undoable transaction.

