- A visible indication of the current drawing or selection mode.

The grid should stay visually consistent while zooming.

Avoid an excessively dense grid at low zoom. Adjust grid detail according to scale.

Gesture separation

Avoid ambiguous gestures.

A one-finger drag on a selected wall should move the wall or its selected handle, depending on the starting target.

A one-finger drag in the Pan tool should move the viewport.

A two-finger gesture should pan or zoom the viewport without accidentally drawing a wall or moving a selected endpoint.

Pinching must not create walls.

A pinch-to-zoom interaction must not change a selected wall's dimensions.

After a pointer is canceled or lost, release all temporary interaction state and do not commit an unintended operation.

Use Pointer Events and pointer capture appropriately.

Apply suitable "touch-action" settings to the drawing viewport so browser scrolling and gesture handling do not conflict with the editor.

Avoid preventing useful browser behavior globally when only the drawing viewport needs custom interaction.

Coordinate correctness

All drawing and drag operations must use the inverse of the current viewport transform.

Never assume that one CSS pixel equals one world unit.

Snapping must use the current scale.

Test drawing, selecting, dragging, and editing at multiple zoom levels and after panning the viewport.

