13. Stable 3D Isometric View

Use Three.js to construct a genuine 3D representation of the current project.

The 3D view must be visual-only.

Do not display editable wall endpoint handles or allow a user to modify wall geometry by dragging objects in 3D.

Geometry generation

For every model wall, construct a 3D wall mesh using:

- Its two endpoints.
- Its actual length.
- Its height.
- Its thickness.
- Its orientation in the 2D plan.

Map 2D X to 3D X, 2D Y to 3D Z, and architectural height to 3D Y.

The model must use the same units and dimensions as the 2D plan.

Do not approximate every wall as a fixed-length object.

Do not use decorative placeholder geometry instead of the real wall model.

Wall height and thickness must be respected.

The same wall positions must be visible in both views.

Wall intersections should not exhibit obvious gaps caused by an incorrect center point or rotation calculation.

A simple ground plane and subtle reference grid may be included.

The first version does not require a roof, doors, windows, furniture, materials editor, or photorealistic rendering.

Camera implementation

Use a stable orthographic camera for a predictable architectural isometric-style view, with appropriate orbit controls.

Initialize the camera at a useful three-quarter isometric angle.

The camera must have a clearly defined target near the center of the building.

Provide controls for:

- Horizontal orbit.
- Vertical orbit.
- Zoom.
- Pan.
- Fit Model.
- Reset Camera.

The camera must never flip unpredictably or become stuck under the ground plane.

Constrain the vertical orbit to a sensible range.

Prevent accidental uncontrolled camera rotation and sudden jumps.

Set reasonable minimum and maximum zoom values.

Keep the camera's up direction consistent.

Use appropriate damping for smooth motion.

On touchscreens, the camera must respond predictably to one-finger rotation and two-finger gestures, in accordance with the control configuration.

Camera stability rules

These rules are mandatory:

- Do not recreate the WebGL renderer whenever React state changes.
- Do not recreate the camera whenever a wall changes.
- Do not reset the camera whenever the project geometry updates.
- Do not change the camera target on every frame.
- Do not reset the orbit controls when selecting or editing a wall in the 2D plan.
- Do not animate the camera automatically in the background.
- Do not enable auto-rotation by default.

Preserve the user's camera position, target, and zoom when the wall geometry changes.

When the 3D view is opened for the first time, fit the current model into the viewport.

When the user explicitly selects Fit Model, calculate an appropriate framing based on the current building bounds.

When the user selects Reset Camera, return to the defined default architectural view.

Do not let camera-fitting logic run continuously.

Efficient updates

Maintain the Three.js renderer and scene for the lifetime of the 3D view.

Update wall meshes when the geometry model changes.

Dispose of obsolete geometries and materials when they are removed.

Clean up animation loops and event listeners when the 3D view unmounts.

Handle window and viewport resizing correctly.

Avoid memory leaks and duplicate rendering loops.

Keep the implementation efficient on mobile devices.

3D presentation

Use a professional, neutral material palette.

Keep wall silhouettes readable.

Avoid excessive reflections, expensive shadows, heavy post-processing, and unnecessary lighting effects.

A clean, lightweight architectural preview is more important than photorealism.

Provide a useful empty state when no walls have been drawn yet.

14. View Switching and Synchronization

The Plan and 3D View controls must represent different views of the same project.

Switching from Plan to 3D View must not duplicate or transform the actual model.

Switching from 3D View back to Plan must not restore an older copy of the geometry.

After every valid geometry transaction:

1. Validate the updated model.
2. Commit the change.
3. Update the 2D rendering.
4. Update the 3D representation.
5. Update the contextual inspector if necessary.
6. Save the project.
7. Record the operation in undo history.

Do not block normal editing while waiting for an animation.

For larger models, optimize rendering rather than introducing delayed or inconsistent geometry updates.

Do not preserve a separate stale 3D model as application data.

15. Undo and Redo

Implement real undo and redo for geometry operations.

Support at least:

- Create wall.
- Delete wall.
- Move wall.
- Resize wall.
- Rotate wall.
- Change wall thickness.
- Change wall height.
- Split wall at an intersection.
- Connect or disconnect endpoints.

Each completed user action must produce one sensible history entry.

Dragging a wall for one continuous movement must not produce hundreds of undo steps.

Use a transaction approach: capture the relevant original state, perform the interaction, and commit a single operation when the interaction ends.

Undo must restore the exact previous geometry and connections.

Redo must restore the corresponding updated geometry.

Do not mutate previous history snapshots.

Keep undo history project-specific.

Clear temporary drag previews on cancellation without creating committed geometry.

Provide clear Undo and Redo controls, with disabled states when no operation is available.

Support common keyboard shortcuts on desktop where appropriate, while preventing them from interfering with text fields.

